{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 34, :comments ["<b>A day for a year, a day for a year.</b> It should have said 'a year for a day, a year for a day.' And further, is not the measure of good multiplied beyond the measure of punishment, so how could their punishment be doubled, a year for a day? And what is written 'בְּמִסְפַּר הַיָּמִים' — it should have said 'כְּמִסְפַּר' with a כ. And what is the meaning of His returning and saying 'תִּשְׂאוּ עֲוֺנֹתֵיכֶם אַרְבָּעִים שָׁנָה,' when He had already mentioned their punishment? And our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Ta'anit 29a) that this day was the ninth of Av, for on it they wept a weeping for nothing, and they established weeping for the generations; therefore on every day of the ninth of Av they would bury their dead. Therefore it says 'יוֹם לַשָּׁנָה' — that every year you shall bear your iniquities one day." "And by way of derash it appears to me to explain, that the principal intention of the spies was to bring out an evil report of the land in order to have an opening of the mouth to say 'נִתְּנָה רֹאשׁ וְנָשׁוּבָה מִצְרָיְמָה,' and they knew that from aforetime and long ago it had been decreed that Egypt would lie desolate forty years, as it is written in Ezekiel (29:12) 'תִּהְיֶיןָ שְׁמָמָה אַרְבָּעִים שָׁנָה,' and Rashi explained there that because a famine of forty-two years was decreed upon them, corresponding to the three times that the dream was written — seven cows, seven ears — and they had only two of them, for from when Jacob came the famine ceased and they remained liable for another forty years; thus Rashi explained. And according to this there is room for me to explain that the spies too knew that the Egyptians have to pay a debt of forty years — either to be desolate forty years, or to have famine there forty years. Therefore they went forty days, to say to Israel: these forty days in which we saw the danger of a land that devours its inhabitants are worse for us than the forty bad years decreed upon Egypt, and therefore they said 'נָשׁוּבָה מִצְרַיְמָה,' for it is better for us to be in the desolation of Egypt forty years than in those forty days in which we were in danger of death and miscarriage in the land. Therefore it says: 'בְּמִסְפַּר הַיָּמִים אֲשֶׁר תַּרְתֶּם אֶת הָאָרֶץ אַרְבָּעִים יוֹם' — so that a day may be reached corresponding to a year of Egypt, to say that a year of Egypt is better than a day of the land, even a year of famine or desolation. And this is what it says 'יוֹם לַשָּׁנָה יוֹם לַשָּׁנָה,' that is, for a year — a year of Egypt. Therefore 'תִּשְׂאוּ עֲוֹנֹתֵיכֶם אַרְבָּעִים שָׁנָה,' corresponding to those forty bad years of Egypt which you chose to be there in siege and distress rather than a day in the land; therefore you shall be in siege and distress in the wilderness forty years. And this is a precious interpretation."], :book :numbers, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:41:20.670759062Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}